></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.836em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.9805em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0379em;\">δ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0379em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">ρ</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.988em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.1076em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ρ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ρ</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></div><h2 id=\"diagnostics\">Separate local error, its spatial extent and whole-domain error</h2><p>In a fixed physical region including the center, inspect maximum specific-energy excess, minimum density and integrated density error together. Measure the shock layer separately; excluding the center does not establish whole-domain accuracy.</p><p>The saved figure uses the existing 64/128/256/512 grids. Left: specific internal energy within r&lt;=0.45r_s. Center: signed density error on the whole domain. Right: whole-domain L1/L2/Linf. Open the image to zoom. Spherical volume weighting r² downweights a small central defect, so inspect local maxima as well as integral norms.</p><figure class=\"reading-saved-figure\"><a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/results/noh-shock-errors.png\"><img src=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/results/noh-shock-errors.png\" alt=\"Existing saved Noh figure: origin heating, density errors and refinement. Whole-domain errors include the origin and shock. No new PDE run or overheating correction is presented.\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1904\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption>Existing saved Noh figure: origin heating, density errors and refinement. Whole-domain errors include the origin and shock. No new PDE run or overheating correction is presented.</figcaption></figure><h2 id=\"saved-results\">What the saved measurements establish</h2><p>Saved maximum center specific-internal-energy excesses at N=512 in edition 2026-11-23-r5 are 2.58% planar, 8.16% cylindrical-radial and 20.59% spherical-radial. The separate 128² Cartesian calculation in edition 2026-11-23-r2 gives 13.99%. These stored results use different reconstructions, geometries and grids; they are not a controlled ranking of methods.</p><p>The third attempt passed all 105 criteria across the geometries, yet maximum spherical center overheating increased from 15.52% in the second attempt to 20.59%. Passing the gates, improving a whole-domain norm and eliminating wall heating are different claims. Original criteria, failed attempts and saved values are retained.</p><p><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/validation/noh-shock/?lang=en\">Planar/cylindrical/spherical figures, inputs, code and verification</a> · <a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/validation/noh-cartesian-2d/?lang=en\">Actual 2D Cartesian Noh calculation and origin heating</a></p><h2 id=\"conservation\">Global conservation does not rule out local overheating</h2><p>At a stationary impermeable reflecting wall, zero normal velocity gives zero physical Euler total-energy flux. Errors in the local kinetic/internal-energy partition and numerical entropy production can remain even when total energy is conserved. Account separately for the outer inflow of the Noh problem.</p><p>Artificial viscosity, numerical flux, reconstruction and initial stagnation treatment can affect the error. A conduction-based correction needs comparison of density, pressure, shock position, positivity and budgets under the same conditions. This chapter introduces no new correction solver or general elimination guarantee.</p><div class=\"math-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mrow><mtext>at a stationary impermeable reflecting wall</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathcal E}=(\\mathcal E+p)u_n=0\\quad\\hbox{at a stationary impermeable reflecting wall}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathcal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0894em;\">E</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0894em;\">E</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">at a stationary impermeable reflecting wall</span></span></span></span></span></div><h2 id=\"scope\">Sources and the scope checked here</h2><p>Noh (1987) is cited for the problem and wall-heating error. The publisher bibliography and abstract were checked; no full-text audit or reproduction of every original figure is claimed. The pinned LANL ExactPack analytic evaluator independently checks exact states; it is not an independent PDE solver.</p><p>This chapter interprets data in existing public releases. It adds no new PDE execution, acceptance change, physical test problem or execution example.</p><h2 id=\"scoped-examples\">Check fixed inputs: separate scoped releases</h2><p>Check each example’s inputs, observables, diagnostic definitions and scope. What a passing check establishes depends on its acceptance criteria and differs from a general mathematical guarantee. Historical inputs, measurements and criteria are preserved.</p><article class=\"resource-summary\" id=\"supplement-noh-shock\"><h3>Noh shocks: zero-pressure planar, cylindrical and spherical flow</h3><p>Computation for a fixed target</p><p>An original small 1D finite-volume code solves zero-pressure Noh flow in planar, cylindrical and spherical symmetry. Grids 64/128/256/512 measure full-domain error, origin heating, shock location and budgets, and compare spherical boundary-inflow time-quadrature error with its analytic identity.</p><p>An educational calculation: first-order space in planar/cylindrical flow and MUSCL-type limited linear reconstruction in spherical flow. Origin heating and finite-grid plateau errors remain; the center is never removed from whole-domain error. Because r^2 weighting can make central defects appear small, an origin-inclusive fixed-core norm and enlarged view are also shown. Vanishing Linf/center maximum error, high order for the complete method on smooth spherical problems, general stage positivity with the geometric source, Cartesian multidimensional flow and independent arbitrary-gamma validation are not established. Cylindrical Linf was not monotone in the old series; spherical L2/Linf and heating are retained as diagnostics. Roundoff-scale discrete mass/energy balance coexists with O(dt^2) spherical boundary-time-quadrature error against analytic totals. Radial momentum has a geometric source and is not a conserved Cartesian momentum component. ExactPack independently implements the analytic evaluator, not another numerical PDE engine.</p><p><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/validation/noh-shock/?lang=en\">Input, results and acceptance</a> · <a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/en/read/validation-noh/#noh-shock\">Method, equations and sources</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/run.py\">Code: run.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/noh_solver.py\">Code: noh_solver.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/noh_reference.py\">Code: noh_reference.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/generate_exactpack_reference.py\">Code: generate_exactpack_reference.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/plot_noh.py\">Code: plot_noh.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/test_noh.py\">Tests: test_noh.py</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/atlas-noh-2026-11-23-r5.zip\">Release ZIP</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/results/result.json\">Saved results</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r5/noh/verification.json\">Independent-extraction rerun receipt</a></p></article><article class=\"resource-summary\" id=\"supplement-noh-cartesian-2d\"><h3>Noh problem: an actual 2D Cartesian Euler calculation</h3><p>Computation for a fixed target</p><p>A small 2D Euler finite-volume calculation evolves density, both momenta and total energy over the full square. Three grids,32²/64²/128², measure Noh-reference errors, budgets and grid orientation effects. The picture shows the actual evolved 2D array.</p><p>A first-order-space lightweight example retains origin heating and shock errors. Finest maximum center specific internal energy is still about 14% high. Axis/diagonal shock-radius difference is about .501dx and is not monotone over these grids. Roundoff-level D4 parity does not establish exact circular symmetry. General perturbation stability, displaced origins, rotated meshes, 3D Cartesian flow and high-order accuracy are unverified. Earlier planar/cylindrical/spherical 1D editions and gates remain unchanged.</p><p><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/validation/noh-cartesian-2d/?lang=en\">Input, results and acceptance</a> · <a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/en/read/validation-noh-cartesian/#noh-cartesian-2d\">Method, equations and sources</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r2/noh-cartesian/run.py\">Code: run.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r2/noh-cartesian/cartesian_solver.py\">Code: cartesian_solver.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r2/noh-cartesian/cartesian_reference.py\">Code: cartesian_reference.py</a> · <a href=\"https://hrkoyama.github.io/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r2/noh-cartesian/test_cartesian.py\">Tests: test_cartesian.py</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r2/noh-cartesian/atlas-noh-cartesian-2026-11-23-r2.zip\">Release ZIP</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r2/noh-cartesian/results/result.json\">Saved results</a> · <a href=\"/code/validated-examples/2026-11-23-r2/noh-cartesian/verification.json\">Independent-extraction rerun receipt</a></p></article><h2 id=\"publication-scope\">Publication scope and related entries</h2><p>An explanatory chapter preserving the existing Noh releases, figures, inputs, references and verification history. These examples measure wall heating; they do not eliminate it.</p><ul><li><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/methods/rusanov/?lang=en\">Rusanov / local Lax–Friedrichs flux</a></li><li><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/methods/heun/?lang=en\">Heun / SSPRK(2,2)</a></li><li><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/methods/muscl/?lang=en\">MUSCL reconstruction</a></li><li><a href=\"/numerical-methods-atlas/methods/minmod/?lang=en\">Minmod</a></li></ul><h2 id=\"sources\">Primary publications, supporting sources and review scope</h2><p>Distinguish original research, later variants, author monographs, pedagogical articles and official references. Bibliographic verification differs from consulting selected passages; neither implies a full-paper audit or numerical reproduction. Access-depth notes accompany each source.</p><ol class=\"reading-sources\"><li id=\"source-noh1987\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9991(87)90074-X\">W. F. Noh (1987). Errors for calculations of strong shocks using an artificial viscosity and an artificial heat flux</a>. Journal of Computational Physics<p>Research publication: proposal or related formulation · Abstract and bibliography checked · <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/002199918790074X\">Consulted record</a></p><p>Publisher bibliography and abstract; original full text was not accessed. Cited for the cold-inflow problem and excess wall-heating context, not as the source of this finite-volume implementation.</p></li><li id=\"source-thrussell2023exactpack\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.softx.2023.101560\">J. Thrussell and J. M. Ferguson (2023). ExactPack: A python library of exact analytic solutions</a>. SoftwareX<p>Research publication: proposal or related formulation · Selected source passages consulted · <a href=\"https://github.com/lanl/ExactPack/blob/9bacc477eb791e959c9621e771fc7a725db755fa/exactpack/solvers/noh/noh1.py\">Consulted record</a></p><p>Selected official pinned Noh evaluator code, especially gamma=5/3 shock position, density, pressure and specific energy. Reuses existing independent ExactPack checks; no new code execution or full-paper audit.</p></li></ol>",
